<h1>Lights &amp; camera</h1>
<p>Lights make 3D art readable; the camera decides what the player sees and where a click lands in the world. This page covers the three light types, the environment and post effects, cosmetic denting, camera conversions, and transform directions.</p>

<h2>Why lights exist</h2>
<p>Without lights, a 3D scene is flat grey: triangles have no shade, no highlight, no sense of depth. A <strong>light</strong> is a component that tells the renderer where illumination comes from. Three types cover almost every scene, each matching a real-world source.</p>

<h2>The three light types</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>What it imitates</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>DirectionalLight</code></td><td>Distant sun — parallel rays across the whole scene</td><td>Key sunlight on Skyforge exterior decks; one per scene is usually enough.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>PointLight</code></td><td>Bare bulb — shines outward in all directions from a point</td><td>Lamps, consoles, explosion flashes, warning beacons.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>SpotLight</code></td><td>Flashlight — a cone in one direction</td><td>Searchlights, doorways, hangar bay floods, aiming lights.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<pre><code>{
  "DirectionalLight": {
    "color": [1, 0.95, 0.85],
    "intensity": 1.2,
    "casts_shadows": true
  }
}

{
  "PointLight": {
    "color": [0.4, 0.8, 1],
    "intensity": 2.0,
    "range": 8
  }
}

{
  "SpotLight": {
    "color": [1, 1, 1],
    "intensity": 3.0,
    "range": 20,
    "inner_angle": 15,
    "outer_angle": 35
  }
}</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>color</code></td><td>RGB of the emitted light, each 0 to 1.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>intensity</code></td><td>Brightness multiplier. Start near 1 and adjust.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>range</code></td><td>How far the light reaches, in world units. Point and spot only.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>casts_shadows</code></td><td>Enables directional shadows. Point/spot shadow casting is not implemented.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>inner_angle</code> / <code>outer_angle</code></td><td>Angles in degrees from the spotlight axis. Full brightness inside the inner cone, fading at the outer cone.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>The current built-in lighting path supports one directional light plus up to four point and four spot lights per camera. Only the directional light casts shadows. Point and spot lights need <code>Transform3D</code> for their world position; the spotlight's <code>direction</code> is transformed by its hierarchy.</p>

<h2>Environment3D</h2>
<p><code>Environment3D</code> controls the panorama backdrop, ambient color/intensity, fog, anti-aliasing and shadow quality. A sky texture does not by itself provide reflection probes or physically based image lighting.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "Environment3D": {
    "sky_texture": "asset://textures/orbit_sky",
    "ambient_intensity": 0.5,
    "shadow_cascades": 4,
    "shadow_resolution": 1024
  }
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>sky_texture</code> references an equirectangular sky texture asset.</li>
  <li><code>ambient_intensity</code> multiplies <code>ambient_color</code>, independently of the sky image.</li>
  <li>Place one <code>Environment3D</code> in the scene. Outdoors in Skyforge uses an orbital panorama; interiors can use a darker studio image.</li>
</ul>

<h2>PostProcessStack</h2>
<p>For shadow distance, cascades, filtering, bias and their costs, see the <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'gameplay-3d'}) }}#directional-shadows">directional shadow settings</a>. Cascades improve near-camera detail without shrinking the whole scene's shadow range.</p>
<p>Post-processing is the final grade applied to the whole rendered image after the scene is drawn — bloom on neon signs, color grading for a cold station mood, vignette to focus the center.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "PostProcessStack": {
    "bloom": 0.4,
    "bloom_threshold": 0.9,
    "saturation": 1.05,
    "contrast": 1.1,
    "vignette": 0.15
  }
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>bloom</code> is the glow strength; <code>bloom_threshold</code> is the brightness cutoff.</li>
  <li><code>saturation</code> and <code>contrast</code> are numeric fields directly on the stack. Attach the stack to the camera entity.</li>
  <li><code>vignette</code> darkens the corners, 0 to 1.</li>
  <li>Keep effects subtle — a heavy grade fights readability of <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'models-and-meshes'}) }}">models</a> and UI.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Denting meshes</h2>
<p>Some impact should leave a mark without splitting an object apart. <code>Dentable3D</code> opts a mesh into cosmetic deformation: hull plating that bows where a drone struck, cargo pods that bruise on hard landings.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "Dentable3D": {}
}</code></pre>
<p>Drive dents from Lua with <code>demi.mesh.deformation</code>:</p>
<pre><code>local Deform = require("demi.mesh.deformation")

local accepted, error = Deform.dent(id, {
    point = {x = x, y = y, z = z},
    direction = {x = 0, y = 0, z = -1},
    radius = 0.32, depth = 0.08,
})
local accepted, error, depth = Deform.impact(id, contact)
Deform.reset(id)</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Arguments</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>dent</code></td><td><code>id</code> and an impact table containing world-space point, inward direction, radius and depth.</td><td>Boolean accepted, error string.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>impact</code></td><td><code>id</code>, contact record with <code>point_x/y/z</code>, <code>normal_x/y/z</code>, <code>impact_energy</code>; optional material overrides.</td><td>Boolean accepted, error string, dent depth.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>reset</code></td><td><code>id</code></td><td>Boolean success. Restores this instance's original visual mesh.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Denting is visual only. Pair it with <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'physics-events'}) }}">physics contact points</a> for placement and with <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'destruction'}) }}">Destruction</a> when the object should eventually break.</p>

<h2>Camera3D conversions</h2>
<p>Pass viewport-local pixels with a top-left origin, +Y down, and the actual viewport width and height. The functions account for the camera's target offset, up axis, projection and transform hierarchy.</p>
<pre><code>local Camera3D = require("demi.camera3d")
local Physics = require("demi.physics.query3d")

local ray = Camera3D.screen_ray(camera_id, mx, my, view_w, view_h)
if ray then
    local origin, direction = ray.origin, ray.direction
    local hit = Physics.raycast(
        origin.x, origin.y, origin.z,
        direction.x, direction.y, direction.z, 100)
    if hit then
        -- hit.entity_id, hit.point, hit.normal, hit.distance
    end
end

local screen = Camera3D.world_to_screen(camera_id, px, py, pz, view_w, view_h)
if screen then
    -- screen.x and screen.y are label coordinates.
end

local wx, wy, wz = Camera3D.screen_to_world(
    camera_id, mx, my, view_w, view_h, 10)
-- World point 10 metres along the normalized screen ray.</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>screen_ray</code> returns origin/direction vectors, not a physics hit. It returns nil if the camera or transform cannot be resolved.</li>
  <li><code>world_to_screen</code> returns a Vec2 table or nil outside near/far depth. Offscreen X/Y coordinates are not clipped.</li>
  <li><code>screen_to_world</code> returns three world-space scalars. Its final distance argument is measured along the ray, not along the camera's forward axis.</li>
  <li><code>Physics.raycast</code>'s optional final argument is an ignored entity ID, not a layer filter.</li>
</ul>
<p>FOV is in degrees. <code>orthographic_size</code> is the full visible height in world units.</p>

<h2>Transform3D directions</h2>
<p>Cameras, lights, and characters constantly need “which way am I facing?”. <code>demi.transform3d</code> answers in world space:</p>
<pre><code>local T = require("demi.transform3d")

local x, y, z = T.get_position(id)
local rx, ry, rz = T.get_rotation(id)
local sx, sy, sz = T.get_scale(id)

T.set_position(id, x, y, z)
T.set_rotation(id, rx, ry, rz)     -- Euler radians
T.set_scale(id, sx, sy, sz)
T.add_position(id, dx, dy, dz)

local fx, fy, fz = T.forward(id)
local rxx, ryy, rzz = T.right(id)
local ux, uy, uz = T.up(id)

T.look_at(id, tx, ty, tz)</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Arguments</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>get_position</code> / <code>get_rotation</code> / <code>get_scale</code></td><td><code>id</code></td><td>Three local-space scalars. Each coordinate is <code>nil</code> when the entity or component is missing.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>set_position</code> / <code>set_rotation</code> / <code>set_scale</code></td><td><code>id</code> plus three scalars</td><td>Boolean success. Writes local-space values; rotation is Euler radians.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>add_position</code></td><td><code>id</code>, delta <code>dx, dy, dz</code></td><td>Boolean success. Offsets the current local position.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>forward</code> / <code>right</code> / <code>up</code></td><td><code>id</code></td><td>Three world-space unit-vector components, or <code>nil</code> per component when missing.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>look_at</code></td><td><code>id</code>, target <code>tx, ty, tz</code> in world space</td><td>Boolean success. Aims the entity's +Z axis at the world point, compensating for its parent transform.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>A spotlight that tracks the player:</p>
<pre><code>local T = require("demi.transform3d")

local px, py, pz = T.get_position(player_id)
if px then T.look_at(spot_id, px, py, pz) end</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li>This example assumes the player is a scene root, so its local position is also its world position.</li>
  <li>Set the spotlight's local <code>direction</code> to <code>[0, 0, 1]</code> to match <code>look_at</code>'s +Z axis; its default is -Z.</li>
  <li>Hierarchy is respected, so a spotlight parented to a moving gantry still aims correctly.</li>
</ul>
<p>Getters return separate numbers and <code>nil</code> per coordinate when the entity or component is missing — guard before arithmetic.</p>

<h2>Scene setup checklist</h2>
<ul>
  <li>One <code>DirectionalLight</code> for the key sun, one <code>Environment3D</code> for the sky and ambient.</li>
  <li><code>PointLight</code> and <code>SpotLight</code> only where the design calls for them.</li>
  <li>One <code>PostProcessStack</code> with restrained settings.</li>
  <li>One camera; conversions always take the real viewport size.</li>
  <li><code>Transform3D.look_at</code> for aiming, world vectors for facing checks.</li>
</ul>
